From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 10:54:28 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] api: add InspectVethWire, a read-only wire verdict that fails
 to "unknown"

EnsureVethWire (0005) answers "make this wire correct". A reconciler must
first answer a different and strictly weaker question -- "IS this wire
correct?" -- with no side effects, and it cannot reuse EnsureVethWire's
answer, because the two have opposite defaults.

EnsureVethWire runs inside a CNI ADD. The sandbox is new, nothing is
carrying traffic, and recreating a wire it cannot verify costs nothing, so
decideEnsureVeth folds every unproven case into `recreate` -- including the
case where the reciprocal veth-pair check simply could not be performed. A
background reconciler runs against a LIVE lab. There, recreating a wire it
merely failed to verify is far worse than the fault it went looking for: it
takes down an adjacency that was carrying traffic.

So decideInspectVeth is a separate decision with the burden of proof
inverted. WireHealthBroken requires positive evidence:

  * an end that is ABSENT while its namespace is enterable, or
  * two ends that exist but are demonstrably NOT the two ends of one veth
    pair -- each side's kernel-reported peer ifindex must equal the other
    side's own ifindex; ifindexes are per-namespace, so the reciprocal
    requirement is what makes the check mean anything, or
  * an end occupied by something that is not a veth at all.

Everything else is WireHealthUnknown and the caller must not act on it: a
namespace that cannot be entered (returned with a *NamespaceGoneError so a
caller can tell "the pod is going away" from "the wire is dead"), a pair
check that could not be performed, a protected ifname. A slot owned by a
DIFFERENT wire's alias is WireHealthConflict -- a configuration error for a
human, never an automatic repair.

InspectVethWire takes no creation lock. It writes nothing, and a verdict
raced by a concurrent ADD is Unknown or Broken, both of which the caller is
required to confirm on a later pass before acting.

Why upstream wants this: koko can already realize a wire idempotently but has
no way to ASK about one, so every caller that wants to know must either
re-run the mutating path or reimplement observation against netlink. This is
the missing read half of the same API, and it is what makes a safe
reconciler possible on top of koko at all.

Positive control: a table over every observation shape, plus
TestDecideInspectVethIsConservativeWhereEnsureIsNot, which pins the exact
disagreement -- for identical observations decideEnsureVeth must say
"recreate" and decideInspectVeth must say "unknown". Executed negative
control: rewriting decideInspectVeth to delegate to decideEnsureVeth fails
both that test ("= broken ... want unknown") and the "unverifiable pair is
unknown" row ("= ok ... want unknown"), i.e. it catches the collapse in both
directions.
